===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|set theory|of a set S}} The [[set]] whose [[element]]s comprise all the [[subset]]s of ''S'' (including the [[empty set]] and ''S'' itself).
#: {{ux|en|The '''power set''' of <math>\{1, 2\}</math> is <math>\left \{\empty, \{1\}, \{2\}, \{1, 2\}\right \}</math>.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Arindama Singh|title=Elements of Computation Theory|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=gqWMGR1otqoC&pg=PA16&dq=%22power+set%22%7C%22power+sets%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwirkJbr0I_cAhWbd94KHZy8BeEQ6AEIkQIwKg#v=onepage&q=%22power%20set%22%7C%22power%20sets%22&f=false|page=16|publisher=Springer
|passage=Moreover, for notational convenience, we write the cardinality of a denumerable set as <math>\aleph_0</math>. Cardinality of the '''power set''' of a denumerable set is written as <math>\aleph_1</math>. We may thus extend this notation further by taking cardinality of the '''power set''' of the '''power set''' of a denumerable set as <math>\aleph_2</math>, etc. but we do not have the need for it right now.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=A. Carsetti|title=Epistemic Complexity and Knowledge Construction|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=ueREAAAAQBAJ&pg=PA94&dq=%22power+set%22%7C%22power+sets%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwirkJbr0I_cAhWbd94KHZy8BeEQ6AEISzAH#v=onepage&q=%22power%20set%22%7C%22power%20sets%22&f=false|page=94|publisher=Springer
|passage='''Theorem 4.1.''' ''A complete Boolean algebra B has a set of (complete and atomic) ca-free generators iff B is isomorphic to the '''power set''' of a '''power set'''.''}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Amir D. Aczel|title=Finding Zero: A Mathematician's Odyssey to Uncover the Origins of Numbers|publisher=Palgrave MacMillan|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=nAADBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA147&dq=%22power+set%22%7C%22power+sets%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwirkJbr0I_cAhWbd94KHZy8BeEQ6AEIiQEwEg#v=onepage&q=%22power%20set%22%7C%22power%20sets%22&f=false|page=147
|passage=Exponentiation is essentially a move to the '''''power set'''''—the set of all subsets of a given set. This is one of the reasons why Bertrand Russell's paradox is indeed a paradox: We cannot find a universal set because no set can contain its own '''power set'''!}}

====Usage notes====
The power ''set'' is more properly a {{m|en|family}} of sets (or possibly, in this case, a ''family of subsets''), rather than a [[set]].

Denoted using the notation ''P''(''S'') with any one of several fonts for the letter "P" (usually uppercase). Examples include: <math>\mathcal{P}(S)</math>, <math>\wp(S)</math> (with the ''{{w|Weierstrass p}}''), <math>\mathbb{P}(S)</math> and 𝒫(''S'').<br>
An alternative notation is <math>2^S\!\!</math>, derived from the consideration that a set <math>T</math> in the power set is fully characterised by determining, for each element of <math>S</math>, whether it is or is not in <math>T</math>.

